finish('matrix-multiplication-exponent-two',
 id='TCS-0007',title='Is the matrix multiplication exponent equal to two?',
 area='Algebraic and numerical computation',criterion='tightness',question_type='yes_no',
 formal=r'''Fix the field of complex numbers. Let s(n) be the minimum number of arithmetic gates in a circuit that computes all entries of AB exactly for arbitrary n-by-n matrices A,B. Is it true that $\forall\varepsilon>0\;\exists C_\varepsilon>0\;\forall n\ge2:\ s(n)\le C_\varepsilon n^{2+\varepsilon}$? Equivalently, is $\omega=\inf\{\tau:s(n)=O(n^\tau)\}=2$?''',
 definitions=r'''The inputs are 2n² independent complex variables a_ij,b_ij. The required n² output polynomials are c_ij=sum_{k=1}^n a_ik b_kj. A circuit is a finite directed acyclic graph with binary addition, subtraction, and multiplication gates; any complex constants and unrestricted reuse of intermediate values are allowed. Size counts arithmetic gates, including operations involving constants. Input nodes, constant nodes, and output designations are not gates. No division, order comparisons, branching, approximation, or randomization is permitted. A different circuit may be chosen for every n: this is the standard nonuniform arithmetic-complexity formulation over C. The cost of encoding a complex number in bits is outside this model. The elementary lower bound s(n)>=n² for n>=2 follows because the n² distinct non-input output polynomials require distinct gates.''',
 answer_criterion='Prove the stated bound for every fixed positive epsilon, or prove omega>2. For the negative direction, it suffices to show that for some fixed epsilon>0 no constant C bounds s(n) by C n^(2+epsilon) for all n. A fixed improvement such as omega<2.37 is progress but not a resolution. Equality of the infimum does not require an O(n²) circuit family or an O(n² polylog n) bound.',
 context=[
  paragraph('The usual algorithm computes n² inner products of length n, spending cubic arithmetic work. That computation treats the outputs separately even though they share their input matrices. Strassen’s construction shows that this shared structure can save an asymptotic factor: seven block multiplications replace eight in a two-by-two block product, and recursive use gives exponent log₂7.', 'strassen'),
  paragraph('The question is whether one can keep reducing the extra exponent all the way to zero. Its quantifiers matter. An algorithm for each exponent 2+epsilon is enough, even when its constant depends badly on epsilon. Conversely, improving one decimal place in the current upper bound does not establish the conjecture. Fixing the field and counting exact scalar operations also separates this question from approximate numerical multiplication or hardware throughput.'),
  paragraph('Modern upper bounds encode multiplication in tensors and exploit savings across large tensor powers. A substantial part of their analysis can be expressed as an optimization over parameters. The August 2026 improvement expands that optimization and uses new search procedures to find better parameters; the paper then describes rational verification of the resulting inequalities. That is an arithmetic exponent result, not an empirical speed comparison.', 'optimization'),
  paragraph('There are also lower bounds on what specific tensor methods can achieve. For example, Alman’s universal-method barrier for Coppersmith–Winograd tensors prevents that route from reaching exponent two. It is not a lower bound on all arithmetic circuits for matrix multiplication. The distinction leaves room for different starting tensors or mathematical constructions.', 'barrier')],
 why='Matrix multiplication is a basic operation whose exponent enters many algorithms in linear algebra and graph theory. Determining whether its arithmetic cost can approach the quadratic scale would settle a central question about sharing computation among many related outputs.',
 importance=dict(score=98,method='editorial',assessed_on='2026-09-10',reason='One of the main questions in algebraic algorithms, governing a widely used primitive and many dependent complexity bounds. Both faster constructions and a superquadratic lower bound would have broad consequences.'),
 progress=[
  step('1969','Strassen gives O(n^(log₂7)) arithmetic operations, establishing that cubic multiplication is not optimal.','strassen'),
  step('2018–2019','Alman proves that the universal method applied to any Coppersmith–Winograd tensor cannot yield an exponent below 2.16805. This is a limitation of that tensor-based route, not a proof that omega exceeds two.','barrier'),
  step('2025','The SODA paper More Asymmetry Yields Faster Matrix Multiplication gives omega<2.371339 by extending the combination-loss analysis. The original preprint appeared in April 2024.','asymmetry'),
  step('2026-08-17','Dupont and coauthors report omega<2.371177. Section 4 describes rounding the optimized parameters to rationals and verifying directed bounds for logarithms; it says the verification repository is being prepared. This catalogue has not independently replayed that certificate.','optimization'),
  step('2026-08-19','Version 3 of More Asymmetry Yields Faster Matrix Multiplication incorporates the new optimization bound. The remaining gap to two persists.','revision')],
 references=[
  ref('strassen','Gaussian elimination is not optimal','Volker Strassen',1969,'https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02165411','Numerische Mathematik 13, 354–356; recursive seven-product construction'),
  ref('book','Mathematics and Computation','Avi Wigderson',2018,'https://www.math.ias.edu/files/mathandcomp.pdf','March 2018 draft, Section 12.3, Open Problem 12.13; arithmetic-circuit formulation'),
  ref('barrier','Limits on the Universal Method for Matrix Multiplication','Josh Alman',2018,'https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.08731','Abstract and main universal-method barrier; published at CCC 2019'),
  ref('asymmetry','More Asymmetry Yields Faster Matrix Multiplication','Josh Alman; Ran Duan; Virginia Vassilevska Williams; Yinzhan Xu; Zixuan Xu; Renfei Zhou',2025,'https://doi.org/10.1137/1.9781611978322.63','SODA 2025 version, bound 2.371339'),
  ref('optimization','Improving the matrix multiplication exponent with modern optimization and AlphaEvolve','Emilien Dupont; Marvin Eisenberger; Borislav Kozlovskii; Abbas Mehrabian; Francisco J. R. Ruiz; Abigail See; Renfei Zhou; Josh Alman; Virginia Vassilevska Williams; Matej Balog',2026,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.16884v1','Submitted 17 August 2026; Sections 1, 4, and 5'),
  ref('revision','More Asymmetry Yields Faster Matrix Multiplication — August 2026 revision','Josh Alman; Ran Duan; Virginia Vassilevska Williams; Yinzhan Xu; Zixuan Xu; Renfei Zhou',2026,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.16349v3','Version 3, 19 August 2026, revised abstract and bound')],
 status='source_open',status_note='The latest primary upper bound located through 10 September 2026 is the reported omega<2.371177 from August 2026. It leaves the omega=2 question open. The model is exact arithmetic over C; the catalogue has not independently reproduced the numerical certificate.',
 formulation_reviewed_on='2026-09-10')
